WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.000
Film Courage: On one of our videos Choice,
an actor left a comment and this actor put

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.330
“I don’t know how to find work and an
agent won’t work with me because I don’t

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:10.400
have experience.”

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.900
What would you tell this actor?

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.090
Choice Skinner, Actor, Acting Teacher and
Director: Pursue a manager because it seems

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:19.120
like nowadays they’re easier to gain.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.880
But…it’s not like how it used to be.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.390
So when you get a manager, you get an agent
then you go out and audition and stuff.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:32.340
You can do a lot of it yourself, you can build
your body of work by submitting and creating

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.040
content.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:39.910
Actors have this tendency of just waiting
around or if they get an agent or a manager

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:40.910
“Oh…okay.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:41.910
Everything is okay.”

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:42.910
No, it’s not.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:50.830
Agents or managers are like the last person
they see is the last person they remember.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:55.920
So if they met you five people ago (or five
persons ago) now they’re not even thinking

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.450
about you and they have to stay in their mind.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.960
You can do so much more nowadays especially
being non-union because there is a lot of

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.190
projects that are non-union.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:09.550
Actors tend to get caught up in this thing
of my career is not going anywhere unless

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:10.550
I’m doing it.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.640
But then it goes back to that money thing
again.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.500
They get into it to make money and the money
is not the key.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.390
You can make money doing a lot of other things.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:26.610
If you’re in the entertainment industry
(specifically acting), you’re in it in order

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.540
to live your dream.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.170
You’re in it to be able to create great
content that’s going to change lives.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.750
That’s just my opinion.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:40.850
But I would top it off (cap it off) by saying
create your own content because you can even

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:48.310
become SAG that way or SAG eligible that way
or submit, submit, submit.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.710
Submit yourself for anything and everything.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.510
Find out who the casting directors are on
projects.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.390
Let them know “Hey listen, I’m here, I’m
available to do stand-in work.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.730
I’m available to do extra work.”

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:03.909
Because I started out as an extra actor and
it opened up doors for me.

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:07.010
The first extra gig that I got, I got camera
time.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:12.680
I didn’t get any voice work but the camera
was on me over and over again.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.280
So there were little signs that told me to
keep going and that’s what I would say to

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.440
anybody who is an actor.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.120
It’s like don’t just depend on getting
an agent or a manager.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.840
You’ve got your own resources.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:28.000
The Internet now is a hive of different types
of resources to submit yourself.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.230
There are so many casting type of opportunities.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:36.060
You know, listen…you may not become the
lead in the film but at least you get in next

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.880
to the person, next to the person.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:39.280
What’s wrong with that?

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.819
Film Courage: And talking about observation
[earlier], it’s a great way to observe how

00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:51.070
the set works, the feel of it, the protocol,
you can see a lot.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:56.450
Choice Skinner: Extra work, background actors,
whatever you want to call it.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:01.599
My first job on set as an extra was FREEJACK.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:06.620
And I got to meet Emilio Estevez and Rene
Russo.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.500
It doesn’t get any better.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.930
In Atlanta, it was the best weekend ever!

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:19.621
We were there for the Prince concert and after
that we were riding by and we saw this area

00:03:19.621 --> 00:03:21.630
and we were like “What’s all this happening
here?”

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:25.740
And so we went over to the area where the
video village is at…I don’t even know

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.410
how we got over there, we just parked and
walked over there and I’m asking this guy

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.410
“What’s going on?”

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.301
And the was like “They’re filming a movie
with Anthony Hopkins and Rene Russo and Emilio

00:03:34.301 --> 00:03:35.301
Estevez.”

00:03:35.301 --> 00:03:36.301
And we were like “Really!?”

00:03:36.301 --> 00:03:37.301
And he was like “Yeah.”

00:03:37.301 --> 00:03:38.301
And we said “What’s that lady doing?”

00:03:38.301 --> 00:03:40.420
And he was like “She’s looking for extras.”

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:41.420
And I said “What’s an extra?”

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.010
And he said “You get to be in the movie.”

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:47.550
So I didn’t know the particulars for it.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.650
But I just knew this is something cool.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.959
And so I go over they said “Just stand there
and look good because she’ll pick you.”

00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:56.910
So she comes and picks me and my friends.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:02.930
And we are in this movie and this is before
I come out to Los Angeles and do my first,

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:06.450
first work (where I knew exactly what I was
doing).

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:10.980
But where I am at there is this car that keeps
going back and forth and they put us right

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:16.320
there and out pops Rene Russo, out pops out
Emilio Estevez.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.499
We’re standing there, we’re talking, we’re
having a good time, we take pictures.

00:04:21.499 --> 00:04:25.740
Emilio and I are singing HAIR songs (songs
from HAIR).

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.379
It’s crazy and I’m like “This is cool.

00:04:28.379 --> 00:04:29.379
This is great.”

00:04:29.379 --> 00:04:34.710
I get to LA and do my first bit extra work
job.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.870
And it gets to the point where I need to become
SAG.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:39.530
And I’m like “Man, how am I going to do
it, I need vouchers.”

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:41.349
I go look in my box…guess what?

00:04:41.349 --> 00:04:45.270
That voucher that I had gotten from FREEJACK
was a SAG eligible voucher.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.919
So I had been Taft Hartleyed and didn’t
even know it.

00:04:48.919 --> 00:04:51.979
Film Courage: And that was just from driving
by, not calling a casting office?

00:04:51.979 --> 00:04:56.159
Choice Skinner: Driving by, didn’t know
anything about it, it’s all about random

00:04:56.159 --> 00:05:01.759
fortune, stoke of luck, got out, went over
and applied yourself.

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:04.800
And that’s what I think people should do,
I think you should apply yourself.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.509
Don’t be afraid to do extra work.

00:05:06.509 --> 00:05:11.499
Sometimes if it’s meant to be for you, maybe
it will align.

00:05:11.499 --> 00:05:13.210
Managers and agents can’t do that for you.

